// Lane H's policy world, as the artifact a guest binds against (docs/WASM.md §8.2, D21).
//
// The second world this helper speaks, and the shape of the difference is the whole point.
// A capability answers a *message*; a policy answers a *permission request* — the JSON form
// of the request `Ouroboros.Control.Permissions` already builds — with a verdict:
//
//     {"decision": "allow" | "deny" | "ask", "rule": "<why, <= 200 chars>"}
//
// Anything else the engine reads as `ask`. `evaluate` returns a bare string rather than a
// `result<string, string>` because a policy has no failure mode the engine treats differently
// from an unreadable verdict: both are `ask`, and a world that offered two ways to say the same
// thing would be inviting an author to think one of them meant something.
//
// The import list is identical, byte for byte, to `capability.wit`'s, and that is the claim:
// one linker, one host function, two worlds. A policy component decides permissions, so it must
// be *deterministic* — and it is by construction, because there is no clock and no randomness
// here to be nondeterministic with (D20).
//
// `tui/wasm/src/world.rs` hard-codes this same shape, because the helper must be able to refuse
// bytes without a WIT parser on the loading path.

package ouroboros:policy@0.1.0;

world policy {
  /// JSON metadata: name, version. Pure.
  export describe: func() -> string;
  /// Called once per instance with host-supplied JSON config.
  export init: func(config: string) -> result<_, string>;
  /// One permission request in, one verdict out. JSON both ways. A verdict this host cannot
  /// read is `ask`, which is the direction every failure in this lane takes.
  export evaluate: func(request: string) -> string;
  /// Log line into the daemon's logger. The only import, in both worlds.
  import log: func(level: string, message: string);
}
